editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Track counts with zero and exactly one blocked cell visited. At an open cell, add the top and left counts independently for each state. At a blocked cell, only paths that had used zero can enter, and they now enter the one-used state. Seed a single path above the starting cell so a blocked start is handled by the same rule. Sum both states at the end.

## Walk through the sample
The destination is blocked. Of the three routes across the 2 × 3 sample, only down, right, right reaches it without first spending the removal. The other two routes already crossed the blocked top-middle cell.

## Complexity
Time O(nm), space O(m).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;
    constexpr int MOD = 1000000007;
    vector<array<int, 2>> dp(m + 1);
    dp[1][0] = 1;
    for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r) {
        string row;
        cin >> row;
        for (int c = 1; c <= m; ++c) {
            int zero = (dp[c][0] + dp[c - 1][0]) % MOD;
            int one = (dp[c][1] + dp[c - 1][1]) % MOD;
            dp[c] = row[c - 1] == '#' ? array<int, 2>{0, zero} : array<int, 2>{zero, one};
        }
    }
    cout << (dp[m][0] + dp[m][1]) % MOD << '\n';
}
~~~
